WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.979
All right, let's get into audience questions
for the week.

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:03.979
First one.

00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:04.979
Hey David.

00:00:04.979 --> 00:00:10.210
And why is the educational divide among white
voters not there among non white voters?

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.930
This is a really good,

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.290
it may be the wrong question in a sense and
I'll explain that in a minute, but first let's

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.560
discuss what we're talking about here.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.930
We recently did a story which looked at polling
in terms of Trump support.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:27.680
And what you saw was that among black voters,
black voters basically opposed Trump by similar,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.390
uh, in similar proportions regardless of whether
they did or didn't go to college.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:37.809
Hispanic voters are opposed to Trump roughly
equally between Hispanic voters who didn't

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:43.899
go to college and Hispanic voters who did
et cetera within white voters, white voters

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:49.319
who didn't go to college are significantly
more likely to support Trump than white voters

00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:50.569
who did go to college.

00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:57.019
So one way to ask it is, why is there an educational
divide among white voters on supporting Republicans?

00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:04.129
The way it may be backwards is maybe the question
is what is it about white voters where when

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.140
they aren't educated, they are falling for
Republican talking points.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.980
But essentially we're asking the same thing
here.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.659
Now there's a few things that aren't good
answers.

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:20.719
One would be it's merely about religion because
if you look at black voters, black voters

00:01:20.719 --> 00:01:24.079
are a pretty significantly religious group.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:28.189
They should be more conservative than they
are based on religiosity.

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:32.020
If that was sort of the X factor, it doesn't
appear to be.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:39.000
When you look at Hispanic voters, education
doesn't become a dividing line I believe because

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.789
regardless of educational attainment, Hispanic
voters know that Republicans don't have their

00:01:44.789 --> 00:01:46.280
interests in mind.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.289
They don't propose policies that are good
for them and Hispanic voters regardless of

00:01:51.289 --> 00:01:56.890
education aren't likely to fall for bogus
Republican talking points like the left is

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.630
really the racist party or whatever the case
may be.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:04.770
The educational element becomes sort of secondary.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:10.970
With a lot of these groups with white voters,
the educational differences become more of

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:18.110
a dividing line because nothing else is bringing
educated and uneducated voters together on

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.110
the same basis.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:23.800
What I mean by that is I've explained to you
many times before, those lame Republican talking

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:29.030
points that don't work on Hispanic voters
regardless of education and they don't work

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:34.330
on black voters regardless of education because
they understand the political realities of

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.220
the Republican party.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:43.610
Those talking points do work on white voters
who are not by virtue of their identity already,

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:48.680
uh, understanding Republican versus democratic
policy.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.290
And because they are uneducated, they're more
likely to fall for those talking points since

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:57.260
there is no other sort of insulating factor
that will protect them from it.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.740
And this becomes a self reinforcing feedback
loop where in great part, the Republican party

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.530
has written off a lot of these minority groups.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:11.020
They've written off, uh, Hispanic voters and
black voters and Asian voters in many ways

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.070
because they see that their policy isn't appealing.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:18.500
So they're effectively doubling down and trying
to appeal to white voters even more.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.230
That, of course leads to a bigger divide because
the white voters who are educated have more

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:30.470
of a framework with which to understand and
realize that they, there are attempts to bamboozle

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.220
them, whereas the educated uneducated voters
are not.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:39.530
So we have a combination here of a self reinforcing
feedback loop where in Republicans are particularly

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:45.440
targeting white voters and succeeding more
with those that are uneducated, combined with

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.800
the reality that in a lot of these other groups
there is something else about their place

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.340
in the political mill.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:58.050
You, for lack of a better term, that reduces
the importance of what, whether they are or

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.050
are not educated.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:04.070
I mean, as a very micro level, um, I know
a lot of Argentinians here in Boston, I was

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.940
born in Argentina, as many of you know.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:13.870
And in the group there are drastic financial
differences where there are people who, um,

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:19.180
are, you know, effectively just entering the
workforce or have been in it for awhile but

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:25.220
earn very little money, are on social welfare
programs all the way up to medical and legal

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.840
professionals and successful entrepreneurs,
et cetera.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.070
And across that entire group.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.120
Nobody likes Trump.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:38.430
Everybody understands the role that Trump
plays in furthering the Republican policies

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.210
that are not good for Hispanic voters.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:44.480
Voters who are immigrants from Argentina,
however you wanted to find us.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.750
Many, many Argentinians are white as the term
is, uh, and the educational level and the

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:54.620
class differences that exist within the group
don't really seem to move political views

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.900
one way or the other.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:02.370
If you looked at an equivalent group of native
born American born white people with such

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:07.810
drastically different class and educational
backgrounds, my prediction is you would see

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.620
much more of a spectrum of political view.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.740
So those are some of my ideas.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.000
Let me know what I'm missing.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.280
Um, very important question and an important
one to understand, uh, when looking at the

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:17.900
20, 20 election for sure.

